      "writtenOn": "2024-11-15T09:33:23Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s a nice approach as well. I couldn\u0027t find any obvious flaws in it.\n\nThe only caveat I see is this\u0027ll lead to multiple threads waiting on the same task even if there are other tasks in the parked queue that can be un-parked, which can lead to contention. (Maybe that\u0027s okay) \n\nI wonder if it\u0027s possible to traverse the parked tasks in a circular way, then we can simply keep going to the next task without a latch if available otherwise wait on the current task.",
      "parentUuid": "a25f2977_3d7d0940",
      "revId": "479690f3a5e38aa2b82ea48d64f9e7e4f4c2d5cd",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bb650f0f_4d740793",
        "filename": "java/com/google/gerrit/server/git/WorkQueue.java",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 707,
      "author": {
        "id": 3604707
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-11-15T19:30:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Agreed, and unfortunately, I believe it is quite a bit more complex to do that way. \n\nI also would note that if we are iterating over tasks, and not removing them from the queue, we don\u0027t need a priority queue, we can just use a list.\n\nLastly, I believe there might still be another issue: I think it is possible for an unparked task which just got determined to not be ready to start, to become ready to start by a completing task before the soon to be newly parked task has been added to the parked queue, and thus preventing the completing task from seeing it as parked in time to unpark it, once again leading to a potentially parked task with potentially no running tasks to unpark it later?",
      "parentUuid": "7423291e_a990705f",
      "revId": "479690f3a5e38aa2b82ea48d64f9e7e4f4c2d5cd",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    }
